      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that breakdown. I'm actually looking at a much more complex one where the stem seems to change quite a bit. Can you take a look at \"tütarlapsed\"? I want to see the breakdown of the root, the plural marker, and how the stem modifies.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Tütarlapsed\" is a classic example of how Estonian can be trickier than a simple stack of morphemes, especially when dealing with derivational morphology. The root here is *tüdru* (meaning 'girl'), which is the core lexical element. The stem modification is where the real work happens, as the word is actually built up from a compound structure, which is often more complicated than a simple suffixation.\n\nIt breaks down as *tüdru* (girl) + *-la-* (a diminutive/attributive element, though this is debated in some analysis) + *-s* (plural marker). The final *-ed* is the overt plural marker.\n\nThe stem modification is subtle; the original root *tüdru* undergoes changes to accommodate the subsequent derivational and inflectional suffixes, particularly the addition of the *-la-* element, which changes the phonological shape of the stem before the plural marker is affixed. It's less about a simple stem change and more about how the compounding/derivation process restructures the base form.",
      "source": "target",
